    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Discovery / Cloud Service Discovery", "technique": "T1526", "what_happened": "At 2026-11-18 09:41:06 CET, the Azure Activity Log recorded a Microsoft.Resources/subscriptions/read operation (an 'az account show' call) against subscription deadbeef-1234-5678-90ab-cdef00112233 originating from external IP 91.108.56.190 with User-Agent azure-cli/2.55.0. This is the orientation step of an actor holding a stolen Azure tenant token — confirming which subscription and tenant the token is valid for before acting. Wazuh ingested the Activity Log entry and raised a low-level cloud-audit alert.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh azure-activity integration alert (rule.groups contains 'azure'): rule.description references 'Microsoft.Resources/subscriptions/read' with caller IP 91.108.56.190. Univariate signature: a cloud read operation from a non-corporate IP is the benign-looking pre-step that precedes write operations.", "first_seen": "2026-11-18T09:41:06+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence / Execution — Cloud Administration Command", "technique": "T1651", "what_happened": "At 09:43:21 CET (~2 min after the orientation read), the same IP issued a Microsoft.Automation/automationAccounts/runbooks/write operation against automation account aa-corp-ops in resource group rg-prod-corp — an 'az automation runbook create' for a PowerShell runbook named maintenance-helper-20261118_094112. The runbook description was set to the cosmetic value 'maintenance-20261118_094112' to blend in. The runbook content is an IEX download cradle: it issues Invoke-WebRequest to http://91.108.56.190/p.ps1 and pipes the response into Invoke-Expression — a classic stage-2 fetch-and-execute payload. Azure Defender for Cloud content analysis flagged the runbook body, and Wazuh raised rule 92962 'Azure Automation Runbook create attempt' at TP/critical. The create call returned 403 Forbidden but the Activity Log still recorded the attempt with the full operation, resource and caller principal.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 92962 (rule.groups contains 'azure', rule.mitre.id T1651): rule.description references 'Microsoft.Automation/automationAccounts/runbooks/write', runbook name 'maintenance-helper-20261118_094112', resource group 'rg-prod-corp'. The runbook creation directly follows the subscription-read by the same caller IP — the read confirmed the target, the write attempts persistence.", "first_seen": "2026-11-18T09:43:21+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Execution / Lateral Movement — Cloud Administration Command", "technique": "T1651", "what_happened": "At 09:53:12 CET (~10 min after the runbook write attempt), the same IP issued a Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/runCommand/action operation against VM vm-prod-app-01 in resource group rg-prod-corp — an 'az vm run-command invoke' with command-id RunShellScript executing 'id; hostname; cat /etc/passwd | head -3'. This is a direct T1651 execution primitive: the Azure VM Agent runs the supplied script on the guest OS with SYSTEM/root privileges, no inbound network access required. Wazuh raised rule 92963 'Azure VM RunCommand lateral movement' at TP/high. As with the runbook, the call surface in the Activity Log even though the underlying action returned 403.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 92963 (rule.groups contains 'azure', rule.mitre.id T1651): rule.description references 'Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/runCommand/action', VM 'vm-prod-app-01', resource group 'rg-prod-corp'. Same caller IP as steps 1-2 — the actor pivoted from automation-account persistence to direct VM code execution.", "first_seen": "2026-11-18T09:53:12+0100"}
  ],
